// The tasks conformance package. Its `test/` directory drives `core/tasks`'s
// background half — `scope`, `spawn` and the `Scope` a handler may capture —
// and asserts what the module promises rather than what the platform does.
//
// The promise is that a scope returns when its body and every task spawned into
// it have finished, whatever order they ran in, and that a task spawned after
// the body has returned still runs. Nothing here asserts *when* a task runs or
// how much of it overlaps: that is the platform's business, and it is the same
// line `core/actor` draws.
//
// The order the work runs in is not left to chance either. `core/host/testing`
// already makes it a value a test writes down, because a spawned task is run
// through `Tasks.parallel` — so `tasks()` is program order, `seed(n)` is one
// numbered order, and both answer the same thing.
